WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.844
Welcome aboard.

00:00:02.064 --> 00:00:05.072
This is your Sjöskolan companion for week forty.

00:00:05.292 --> 00:00:11.500
Saga and the gull will help us follow three connected lessons: alternating voltage, impedance, and power.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.365
We begin with what changes during one cycle.

00:00:14.585 --> 00:00:18.980
Then we connect the timing of voltage and current to the current drawn by a load.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.701
Finally, we explain what that means for generators, cables and useful power.

00:00:23.921 --> 00:00:26.887
Keep your presentation and calculator beside you.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.200
Last week, a battery gave us a largely steady voltage.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.708
An alternating voltage changes with time and reverses polarity.

00:00:36.928 --> 00:00:39.317
Follow the marker through one sine wave.

00:00:39.537 --> 00:00:45.788
It rises from zero, reaches a positive peak, returns to zero, reaches a negative peak, and returns again.

00:00:46.008 --> 00:00:48.248
That complete pattern is one cycle.

00:00:48.468 --> 00:00:50.943
The curve is a record of voltage over time.

00:00:51.163 --> 00:00:54.000
It does not show a particle travelling along the cable.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.075
The period, capital T, is the time taken for one complete cycle.

00:01:00.295 --> 00:01:04.028
Frequency, f, counts cycles per second and is measured in hertz.

00:01:04.248 --> 00:01:06.317
These quantities are reciprocals.

00:01:06.537 --> 00:01:12.703
At fifty hertz, one divided by fifty is zero point zero two seconds, or twenty milliseconds.

00:01:12.923 --> 00:01:16.805
At sixty hertz, the period is about sixteen point seven milliseconds.

00:01:17.025 --> 00:01:22.359
Measure between matching points on successive cycles, such as two rising zero crossings.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.060
We need three different descriptions of voltage.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.376
The instantaneous value tells us the voltage at one particular moment.

00:01:31.596 --> 00:01:35.116
The peak value is the largest magnitude reached by the wave.

00:01:35.336 --> 00:01:39.219
Peak to peak is the distance from the negative peak to the positive peak.

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:43.151
For a sine wave centred on zero, peak to peak is twice the peak.

00:01:43.371 --> 00:01:47.040
Read the symbol and the description carefully before choosing a formula.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.325
R M S stands for root mean square.

00:01:51.545 --> 00:01:53.935
Its practical meaning is equal heating.

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:56.629
Connect twelve volts D C to a resistor.

00:01:56.849 --> 00:02:01.415
Now compare a sine wave of twelve volts R M S across the same resistance.

00:02:01.635 --> 00:02:04.237
Both deliver the same average heating power.

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:09.769
The alternating voltage changes constantly, so its peak must be higher than twelve volts.

00:02:09.989 --> 00:02:14.640
R M S gives us a useful steady value for comparing the electrical effect.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:22.751
For a pure sine wave with no D C offset, divide the peak by the square root of two to find R M S.

00:02:22.971 --> 00:02:26.661
Going the other way, multiply R M S by the square root of two.

00:02:26.881 --> 00:02:33.324
A two hundred and thirty volt R M S sine wave therefore has a peak of about three hundred and twenty five volts.

00:02:33.544 --> 00:02:37.213
Its peak to peak value is about six hundred and fifty one volts.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.155
Here is the presentation example.

00:02:41.375 --> 00:02:45.855
A sine wave has thirty volts peak to peak and a period of ten milliseconds.

00:02:46.075 --> 00:02:49.147
First, halve thirty to obtain a peak of fifteen volts.

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:52.055
Next, divide fifteen by the square root of two.

00:02:52.275 --> 00:02:55.645
The R M S value is about ten point six one volts.

00:02:55.865 --> 00:02:59.897
Finally, convert ten milliseconds to zero point zero one seconds.

00:03:00.117 --> 00:03:03.189
One divided by that period gives one hundred hertz.

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:07.228
Notice that peak to peak is the largest of these voltage values.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.227
Over a complete cycle, the positive and negative areas of a centred sine wave cancel.

00:03:14.447 --> 00:03:16.879
Its ordinary average voltage is zero.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:18.891
Heating behaves differently.

00:03:19.111 --> 00:03:24.508
Resistor power depends on voltage squared, so both polarities contribute positive heating.

00:03:24.728 --> 00:03:30.211
This is why an alternating supply can warm a heater even though the average voltage is zero.

00:03:30.431 --> 00:03:35.721
Keep average voltage, R M S voltage, and average power as three separate ideas.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.852
The worked example in your slides uses a ten volt peak at twenty five hertz.

00:03:42.072 --> 00:03:44.525
Find the voltage after ten milliseconds.

00:03:44.745 --> 00:03:47.753
Convert the time to zero point zero one seconds.

00:03:47.973 --> 00:03:54.288
Two times pi times twenty five times zero point zero one gives pi divided by two radians.

00:03:54.508 --> 00:03:59.009
The sine of that angle is one, so the instantaneous voltage is ten volts.

00:03:59.229 --> 00:04:02.109
This is one quarter of the forty millisecond period.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.291
To calculate an instantaneous value, use the peak as the amplitude of the sine function.

00:04:09.511 --> 00:04:13.863
In this example the peak is seventeen volts and the frequency is fifty hertz.

00:04:14.083 --> 00:04:18.093
At five milliseconds, the angle is pi divided by two radians.

00:04:18.313 --> 00:04:21.919
The sine is one, so the voltage is positive seventeen volts.

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:25.957
At fifteen milliseconds, the angle is three pi divided by two.

00:04:26.177 --> 00:04:29.761
The sine is minus one, giving negative seventeen volts.

00:04:29.981 --> 00:04:32.691
Set the calculator to radians for this formula.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:38.745
Phase describes where one repeating signal lies relative to another.

00:04:38.965 --> 00:04:42.613
One complete period corresponds to three hundred and sixty degrees.

00:04:42.833 --> 00:04:47.847
At fifty hertz, a five millisecond difference is one quarter of the twenty millisecond period.

00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:49.816
That means ninety degrees.

00:04:50.036 --> 00:04:52.916
A two millisecond difference gives thirty six degrees.

00:04:53.136 --> 00:04:55.333
The size of the gap tells us the angle.

00:04:55.553 --> 00:05:00.503
To say which signal leads, we must also see which one reaches the matching point first.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.551
Compare two signals with the same twelve volt peak.

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:07.328
One is a sine wave.

00:05:07.548 --> 00:05:13.137
The other is a square wave that spends equal time at positive twelve and negative twelve volts.

00:05:13.357 --> 00:05:15.555
Both have zero average voltage.

00:05:15.775 --> 00:05:18.889
The sine has about eight point four nine volts R M S.

00:05:19.109 --> 00:05:24.165
The square wave has twelve volts R M S, because its squared voltage stays constant.

00:05:24.385 --> 00:05:28.311
Always identify the waveform before using the sine-wave conversion.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.700
A centred sine wave measures twenty volts peak to peak.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.160
One cycle lasts four milliseconds.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:39.943
What are its peak voltage, R M S voltage and frequency?

00:05:40.163 --> 00:05:43.192
Pause the film and write the units beside each result.

00:05:49.412 --> 00:05:51.183
The peak is ten volts.

00:05:51.403 --> 00:05:57.568
The R M S value is ten divided by the square root of two, about seven point zero seven volts.

00:05:57.788 --> 00:06:03.676
The period is zero point zero zero four seconds, so the frequency is two hundred and fifty hertz.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.965
Our second lesson adds inductors and capacitors.

00:06:10.185 --> 00:06:13.236
A resistor converts electrical energy into heat.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:16.827
An ideal inductor stores energy in a magnetic field.

00:06:17.047 --> 00:06:20.588
An ideal capacitor stores energy in an electric field.

00:06:20.808 --> 00:06:23.752
The last two can return stored energy to the circuit.

00:06:23.972 --> 00:06:26.148
Their response depends on frequency.

00:06:26.368 --> 00:06:29.333
We describe this with reactance, measured in ohms.

00:06:29.553 --> 00:06:34.375
Impedance combines resistance and reactance, including their phase relationship.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.100
Begin with a resistor.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.477
Current follows voltage immediately in this ideal model.

00:06:41.697 --> 00:06:44.215
Their zero crossings and peaks occur together.

00:06:44.435 --> 00:06:46.504
We say the signals are in phase.

00:06:46.724 --> 00:06:51.993
The two curves use separate amplitude scales because volts and amperes are different quantities.

00:06:52.213 --> 00:06:54.219
Their timing is what matters here.

00:06:54.439 --> 00:06:58.279
As the reference arrows rotate, they point in the same direction.

00:06:58.499 --> 00:07:00.867
Their angle difference is zero degrees.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.008
An ideal inductor opposes changes in current.

00:07:06.228 --> 00:07:10.580
The voltage across it is greatest when current is changing most rapidly.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.664
Current therefore lags voltage by ninety degrees in a sinusoidal steady state.

00:07:15.884 --> 00:07:19.105
Watch the voltage reach its positive peak before the current.

00:07:19.325 --> 00:07:26.152
A real coil also has resistance, so a practical coil does not generally produce a full ninety degree phase difference.

00:07:26.372 --> 00:07:29.743
The ideal model helps us understand its reactive part.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:36.449
Calculate the reactance of an ideal zero point one henry inductor at fifty hertz.

00:07:36.669 --> 00:07:39.677
Multiply two, pi, fifty and zero point one.

00:07:39.897 --> 00:07:43.076
The result is approximately thirty one point four ohms.

00:07:43.296 --> 00:07:48.544
If the frequency doubles while inductance stays the same, inductive reactance doubles as well.

00:07:48.764 --> 00:07:52.284
This does not mean the coil has acquired more ordinary resistance.

00:07:52.504 --> 00:07:58.200
We are describing the relationship between sinusoidal voltage and current caused by its inductance.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.091
A capacitor behaves differently.

00:08:02.311 --> 00:08:07.601
Current flows as its stored charge changes, and its voltage builds as charge accumulates.

00:08:07.821 --> 00:08:13.496
In sinusoidal steady state, current leads voltage by ninety degrees for an ideal capacitor.

00:08:13.716 --> 00:08:15.679
The current peak arrives first.

00:08:15.899 --> 00:08:19.355
Compare this directly with the inductor, where current lagged.

00:08:19.575 --> 00:08:21.687
These statements describe timing.

00:08:21.907 --> 00:08:26.792
They do not mean that a capacitor creates energy or anticipates a future change.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:36.936
For a one hundred microfarad capacitor at fifty hertz, first convert the capacitance to one hundred times ten to the minus six farads.

00:08:37.156 --> 00:08:42.660
Calculate the complete denominator: two times pi times frequency times capacitance.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:44.907
Then divide one by that result.

00:08:45.127 --> 00:08:49.073
Capacitive reactance is approximately thirty one point eight ohms.

00:08:49.293 --> 00:08:52.728
At one hundred hertz it falls to about fifteen point nine ohms.

00:08:52.948 --> 00:08:57.983
Increasing frequency has the opposite effect on capacitive and inductive reactance.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.056
Resistance and reactance do not point in the same direction on an impedance diagram.

00:09:05.276 --> 00:09:09.116
Draw resistance horizontally, then inductive reactance upwards.

00:09:09.336 --> 00:09:11.341
The impedance is the diagonal.

00:09:11.561 --> 00:09:18.111
With thirty ohms of resistance and forty ohms of inductive reactance, Pythagoras gives fifty ohms of impedance.

00:09:18.331 --> 00:09:21.637
Adding thirty and forty directly would give the wrong answer.

00:09:21.857 --> 00:09:29.111
With one hundred volts R M S across this series circuit, current is one hundred divided by fifty, or two amperes.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.944
Now use the smaller example from the presentation.

00:09:34.164 --> 00:09:40.820
Resistance is twelve ohms, inductive reactance is sixteen ohms, and the supply is sixty volts R M S.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.645
The impedance is twenty ohms, giving a current of three amperes.

00:09:44.865 --> 00:09:49.644
Multiplying current by resistance gives thirty six volts across the resistor.

00:09:49.864 --> 00:09:55.027
Multiplying current by inductive reactance gives forty eight volts across the inductor.

00:09:55.247 --> 00:09:59.300
We must account for their different phases when combining these voltages.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.145
The resistor voltage is in phase with current.

00:10:04.365 --> 00:10:07.587
The inductor voltage leads current by ninety degrees.

00:10:07.807 --> 00:10:10.516
Their voltage arrows are therefore perpendicular.

00:10:10.736 --> 00:10:16.987
The supply is the vector sum: the square root of thirty six squared plus forty eight squared, which is sixty volts.

00:10:17.207 --> 00:10:19.276
Kirchhoff has not stopped working.

00:10:19.496 --> 00:10:22.504
Instantaneous voltages still add around the loop.

00:10:22.724 --> 00:10:28.377
It is the separate R M S magnitudes that cannot simply be added as ordinary positive numbers.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:35.940
To find the impedance angle, take the inverse tangent of reactance divided by resistance.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.957
Use degrees on your calculator when you want the answer in degrees.

00:10:40.177 --> 00:10:45.169
Thirty ohms and positive forty ohms give positive fifty three point one degrees.

00:10:45.389 --> 00:10:49.272
Voltage leads current by that angle, so current lags voltage.

00:10:49.492 --> 00:10:52.436
We use current as the reference in this series circuit.

00:10:52.656 --> 00:10:57.435
Keeping the reference clear prevents the common mistake of reversing the meaning of the sign.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:06.220
For the capacitor example, resistance is twelve ohms and capacitive reactance has a magnitude of sixteen ohms.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:09.021
Write the net reactance as negative sixteen.

00:11:09.241 --> 00:11:12.036
The impedance magnitude remains twenty ohms.

00:11:12.256 --> 00:11:15.029
At sixty volts the current remains three amperes.

00:11:15.249 --> 00:11:18.535
But the angle is now negative fifty three point one degrees.

00:11:18.755 --> 00:11:22.872
The reactive arrow points downwards, and current leads the supply voltage.

00:11:23.092 --> 00:11:27.103
Equal impedance magnitudes do not mean equal phase relationships.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.603
The frequency sweep shows the two trends together.

00:11:31.823 --> 00:11:34.361
Keep the inductance and capacitance fixed.

00:11:34.581 --> 00:11:39.851
As frequency rises, inductive reactance increases and capacitive reactance decreases.

00:11:40.071 --> 00:11:42.951
Their crossing marks equal reactance magnitudes.

00:11:43.171 --> 00:11:46.605
In a series circuit, use their difference as the net reactance.

00:11:46.825 --> 00:11:49.556
Below that crossing, the circuit is capacitive.

00:11:49.776 --> 00:11:51.931
Above it, the circuit is inductive.

00:11:52.151 --> 00:11:55.607
The curves are calculated from the same equations we have just used.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:04.284
At series resonance, inductive and capacitive reactances have equal magnitude and cancel in the net impedance.

00:12:04.504 --> 00:12:09.240
Consider twenty ohms of resistance, with both reactances equal to forty ohms.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:13.129
A one hundred volt R M S supply produces five amperes.

00:12:13.349 --> 00:12:16.763
Each reactive component then has two hundred volts across it.

00:12:16.983 --> 00:12:19.948
Those voltages oppose one another in the vector sum.

00:12:20.168 --> 00:12:26.056
Resonance does not mean that every component voltage disappears, and resistance still limits current.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:35.637
Here is a series circuit with thirty ohms of resistance, fifty ohms of inductive reactance and ten ohms of capacitive reactance.

00:12:35.857 --> 00:12:38.545
The supply is one hundred volts R M S.

00:12:38.765 --> 00:12:42.179
Find the net reactance, impedance magnitude and current.

00:12:42.399 --> 00:12:44.681
Decide whether current leads or lags.

00:12:44.901 --> 00:12:46.309
Pause here.

00:12:52.529 --> 00:12:55.047
The net reactance is positive forty ohms.

00:12:55.267 --> 00:12:58.723
The impedance is fifty ohms, so current is two amperes.

00:12:58.943 --> 00:13:02.036
The circuit is inductive, and current lags voltage.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:09.105
Our final chapter connects these phase relationships to power.

00:13:09.325 --> 00:13:12.269
Imagine a generator supplying an onboard load.

00:13:12.489 --> 00:13:18.719
Multiplying R M S voltage by R M S current gives apparent power, measured in volt amperes.

00:13:18.939 --> 00:13:21.584
It does not always give active power in watts.

00:13:21.804 --> 00:13:24.705
For a sinusoidal load, the phase difference matters.

00:13:24.925 --> 00:13:30.792
At two hundred and thirty volts and ten amperes, apparent power is two thousand three hundred volt amperes.

00:13:31.012 --> 00:13:34.084
We still need the power factor to calculate active power.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.504
Active power is the average rate at which electrical energy becomes work and heat in the load.

00:13:41.724 --> 00:13:47.164
For sinusoidal voltage and current, multiply apparent power by the cosine of the phase angle.

00:13:47.384 --> 00:13:55.085
With two thousand three hundred volt amperes and a power factor of zero point eight, active power is one thousand eight hundred and forty watts.

00:13:55.305 --> 00:13:58.527
On the graph, instantaneous power varies during the cycle.

00:13:58.747 --> 00:14:01.008
The horizontal line marks its average.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:09.208
An ideal reactive component stores energy during part of the cycle and returns it during another part.

00:14:09.428 --> 00:14:11.881
Reactive power describes this exchange.

00:14:12.101 --> 00:14:13.787
It is measured in vars.

00:14:14.007 --> 00:14:20.065
In our convention, inductive reactive power is positive and capacitive reactive power is negative.

00:14:20.285 --> 00:14:26.877
For the previous sinusoidal example, cosine phi is zero point eight, so sine phi is zero point six.

00:14:27.097 --> 00:14:33.476
Reactive power is two thousand three hundred times zero point six, or one thousand three hundred and eighty vars.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:41.303
For sinusoidal conditions, active and reactive power form perpendicular sides of the power triangle.

00:14:41.523 --> 00:14:43.571
Apparent power is the diagonal.

00:14:43.791 --> 00:14:47.396
Three kilowatts and four kilovars give five kilovolt amperes.

00:14:47.616 --> 00:14:53.973
Power factor is active power divided by apparent power: three divided by five, or zero point six.

00:14:54.193 --> 00:14:55.943
Keep the units distinct.

00:14:56.163 --> 00:15:02.200
Watts describe active power, vars describe reactive power, and volt amperes describe apparent power.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:10.955
The presentation example uses nine hundred watts of active power and twelve hundred vars of inductive reactive power.

00:15:11.175 --> 00:15:14.887
The square root of their squared sum is fifteen hundred volt amperes.

00:15:15.107 --> 00:15:20.120
Dividing nine hundred by fifteen hundred gives a power factor of zero point six.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:25.268
At two hundred and thirty volts, supply current is about six point five two amperes.

00:15:25.488 --> 00:15:29.328
Check the result by multiplying voltage, current and power factor.

00:15:29.548 --> 00:15:32.364
You should recover approximately nine hundred watts.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.185
Power describes a rate.

00:15:36.405 --> 00:15:39.072
Energy includes how long that rate continues.

00:15:39.292 --> 00:15:45.564
A load using one point six kilowatts for three hours consumes four point eight kilowatt hours of active energy.

00:15:45.784 --> 00:15:51.053
Even if its apparent power is two kilovolt amperes, use active power for this calculation.

00:15:51.273 --> 00:15:54.111
Keep kilowatts with hours to obtain kilowatt hours.

00:15:54.331 --> 00:15:59.984
Do not confuse a power rating with the accumulated energy recorded over a period of operation.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:08.315
A suitably selected capacitor can supply part of the reactive exchange required by an inductive load.

00:16:08.535 --> 00:16:12.353
Consider a load taking three kilowatts and positive four kilovars.

00:16:12.573 --> 00:16:19.144
In the ideal model, a capacitor with negative four kilovars cancels the reactive demand seen by the supply.

00:16:19.364 --> 00:16:22.713
The required compensation magnitude is four kilovars.

00:16:22.933 --> 00:16:26.795
Apparent power at the supply falls from five to three kilovolt amperes.

00:16:27.015 --> 00:16:29.745
The load still needs three kilowatts of active power.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:41.271
To calculate a compensation requirement for sinusoidal conditions, multiply active power by the difference between the tangents of the original and target phase angles.

00:16:41.491 --> 00:16:48.829
For three kilowatts, improving cosine phi from zero point six to one gives a compensation magnitude of four kilovars.

00:16:49.049 --> 00:16:54.681
Write the capacitor contribution as negative four kilovars in the signed reactive-power balance.

00:16:54.901 --> 00:17:01.323
This distinction explains why a compensation requirement may be written as a positive number in a specification.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:09.239
With active power and supply voltage unchanged, a higher power factor means less supply current.

00:17:09.459 --> 00:17:14.131
Use the presentation example of twelve hundred watts at two hundred and forty volts.

00:17:14.351 --> 00:17:18.105
At a power factor of zero point five, current is ten amperes.

00:17:18.325 --> 00:17:21.013
At a power factor of one, it is five amperes.

00:17:21.233 --> 00:17:24.327
The current has halved, but the useful load has not halved.

00:17:24.547 --> 00:17:28.941
We have reduced the reactive burden on the supply in this ideal sinusoidal model.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.285
Cable heating depends on current squared times resistance.

00:17:34.505 --> 00:17:41.716
If current falls from ten amperes to five amperes in the same cable, the loss ratio is five divided by ten, squared.

00:17:41.936 --> 00:17:44.837
One quarter of the original cable loss remains.

00:17:45.057 --> 00:17:50.220
That is a seventy five per cent reduction in cable heating, under the stated assumptions.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:54.856
It is not a seventy five per cent reduction in the load energy requirement.

00:17:55.076 --> 00:17:57.935
Be precise about what your percentage describes.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.669
Electronic loads can draw distorted current containing harmonics.

00:18:03.889 --> 00:18:08.860
In that case, the phase angle of the fundamental does not describe the entire waveform.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:13.069
Total power factor remains active power divided by apparent power.

00:18:13.289 --> 00:18:19.561
If a meter reports eight hundred watts and one thousand volt amperes, total power factor is zero point eight.

00:18:19.781 --> 00:18:25.563
A fundamental cosine phi reading of zero point nine five can coexist with that result.

00:18:25.783 --> 00:18:29.985
Do not apply the simple power triangle blindly to distorted waveforms.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:37.845
The correction examples are teaching models, not instructions for adding a capacitor to an installation.

00:18:38.065 --> 00:18:42.545
Real systems require suitable equipment, protection and a study of the load.

00:18:42.765 --> 00:18:46.136
Harmonics, resonance and changing demand affect the choice.

00:18:46.356 --> 00:18:50.089
Overcompensation can make the supply see a capacitive load.

00:18:50.309 --> 00:19:00.315
The learning objective is to understand what compensation changes: the reactive demand, apparent power and supply current, while the useful active load can remain the same.

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:09.519
A sinusoidal inductive single-phase load has six hundred watts of active power and eight hundred vars of reactive power.

00:19:09.739 --> 00:19:12.427
Its supply is two hundred volts R M S.

00:19:12.647 --> 00:19:15.697
Find apparent power, power factor and supply current.

00:19:15.917 --> 00:19:18.029
Pause now and draw the triangle.

00:19:24.249 --> 00:19:26.959
Apparent power is one thousand volt amperes.

00:19:27.179 --> 00:19:31.851
Power factor is six hundred divided by one thousand, or zero point six.

00:19:32.071 --> 00:19:36.423
Current is one thousand divided by two hundred, which gives five amperes.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.923
Take three ideas into the week forty exercises.

00:19:43.143 --> 00:19:48.561
First, identify the waveform and distinguish instantaneous, peak and R M S values.

00:19:48.781 --> 00:19:54.157
Second, combine resistance and reactance with their phase relationship, using the correct sign.

00:19:54.377 --> 00:20:00.308
Third, distinguish active, reactive and apparent power, and state when the sinusoidal formulas apply.

00:20:00.528 --> 00:20:02.448
Show your units and assumptions.

00:20:02.668 --> 00:20:06.337
The week forty submission is due on Sunday, the fourth of October.

00:20:06.557 --> 00:20:11.123
Return to the relevant chapter whenever you need to see the relationship move again.
